# Cloudflare Workers static-assets header rules.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# This file is copied verbatim from `public/` to `dist/` by Vite at build
# time; Cloudflare serves it as deploy-time routing metadata, so nothing
# here ships as code to the browser.
#
# THE SCRIPT POLICY (#43 / ET-017). `script-src` is `'self'` plus the
# SHA-256 of each inline boot script in the BUILT `index.html` (today one:
# theme and language/direction before first paint). The build writes those
# hashes in place of the placeholder below and fails if it cannot (see
# packages/lib/src/cspScriptHashes.mjs), and the prerender refuses any
# snapshot carrying an inline script the completed policy does not allow,
# so a page never ships with a script its own policy blocks. There is no
# `'unsafe-inline'` and no `'unsafe-eval'`: nothing on this site evaluates
# text as code, and either keyword would let an injected script run.
#
# WHAT THE POLICY NO LONGER ALLOWS, and why. It was copied from the retired
# connected app and allowed things this site never does:
#   - Google's tag loader and runtime. This site loads no analytics today.
#     Analytics, if added, loads through the consent pipeline
#     (`src/lib/consent.ts`) and is added here as a deliberate entry; its
#     inline consent defaults are then hashed like any other boot script.
#   - Google Fonts. The site loads no web fonts; it uses the device's own.
#   - The WalletConnect verify iframe and WebSockets. This site connects no
#     wallet; it embeds no frame at all (`frame-src 'none'`).
#   - Being framed by Safe's dapp browser. That is the connected app's
#     Safe-App support; a marketing page has no reason to be framed, and
#     `frame-ancestors 'none'` stops it being framed for clickjacking.
#
# THE CONNECT POLICY (#43). `connect-src` is `'self'` plus the one other
# origin the site reads: the indexer whose published protocol-config
# snapshot the pages quote. That origin is build configuration
# (`VITE_INDEXER_ORIGIN`), so the build writes it in place of the second
# placeholder below, through the same rule the pages use to fetch from it
# (`src/lib/indexerOrigin.ts`), and fails if it cannot (see
# packages/lib/src/cspConnectSources.mjs). A script that got onto a page
# can no longer send what it reads to any other host. (`img-src https:`
# is a separate directive this lock does not change.)
#
# `static.cloudflareinsights.com` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT (#1816). If the
# zone has Web Analytics auto-injection on, Cloudflare inserts its RUM
# beacon into every HTML page response — not into assets, images or
# other non-HTML payloads — and this CSP refuses it, which shows up as
# a console error on every page load. The fix is to turn the injection
# OFF at the zone — NOT to add the host here. That beacon is injected at
# the edge, downstream of the app, so `lib/consent.ts` cannot gate it:
# allowing it would run an analytics collector before the visitor has
# consented to any, which the privacy spec forbids outright. The spec
# applies the same rule to the connected app's wallet SDKs' own
# telemetry — a requirement, not a description: neither connector
# switches it off (#1824) — Coinbase reports on every app load,
# WalletConnect is enabled but only forwards events a prior session
# stored — so do not read it here as a shipped precedent.
# Any future analytics — Cloudflare's included — has to load
# through the consent-aware pipeline, and would then be added here as a
# deliberate entry rather than to silence an error.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' __INLINE_SCRIPT_HASHES__; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' __CONNECT_SOURCES__; frame-src 'none'; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# SPA entry point — Cloudflare returns this for every non-asset path via

# wrangler.jsonc `not_found_handling: single-page-application`. Force a

# revalidation on every load so a deploy can never leave a client pinned

# to stale JS references. The JS / CSS bundles themselves stay long-cached

# via the /assets/* rule below because Vite emits content-hashed names.

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Version-pinned Terms archives (#1998 / Codex #2010 r3): tell crawlers

# at the HTTP layer not to index them. The client-side meta tag only

# exists after JavaScript runs; a non-JS crawler requesting /terms/v1

# gets the SPA shell (single-page-application not_found_handling) with

# no directive, and these routes are deliberately absent from the

# sitemap and prerender set — absence does not stop a crawler that

# follows the on-page version-index links. Header rules match the

# REQUEST path, so this attaches to the shell response too.

/terms/v*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# The same archive routes are also mounted beneath the `:locale`

# prefix (App.tsx renders one route tree twice), so /es/terms/v1 etc.

# are equally valid archive URLs and need the same directive — the

# unprefixed rule above does not match them (#2010 r4, verified with

# wrangler dev). `:locale` is a single-segment placeholder; matching

# a non-locale first segment too is harmless, since any

# /<segment>/terms/v* path serves the same shell.

/:locale/terms/v*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# Immutable static assets (Vite emits content-hashed filenames in /assets/*).

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# NOTE: scripts/prerender.mjs APPENDS a generated, marker-delimited
# section of per-route revalidation rules to the built dist/_headers
# (one rule per prerendered entry point, derived from the same
# seo-routes.mjs registry as the snapshots) — the deployed file is
# this one PLUS that section (Codex #1309 r9).
